# Swift/iOS patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' design guidance to Swift concurrency (Swift 6 data-race safety) and SwiftUI state ownership.

## Swift concurrency: async/await and structured concurrency

- Prefer `async`/`await` over completion handlers for new asynchronous code.
- Use `async let` (small fixed number of tasks) or `TaskGroup`/`withThrowingTaskGroup`
  (dynamic set) for concurrent child work, not a bare unstructured `Task {}`.
  `Task.detached {}` is a distinct, rarely needed form; do not use the two names
  interchangeably.
- SwiftUI's `.task { }` closure is already async: do not wrap another `Task {}`
  in it. A bare `Task {}` only at a synchronous-to-async boundary with no async
  closure available (a `Void` button action, a delegate callback), with an
  explicit reason it need not be joined. Prefer `.task { }` when the work is
  tied to the view's lifetime.
- Long-running async work checks `Task.isCancelled` or calls
  `try Task.checkCancellation()` in a loop.

## Actors and @MainActor

- Use `actor` to protect mutable state touched by multiple tasks.
- Since the iOS 18 SDK, `View` is `@MainActor`-isolated, which does not extend to
  a separate type: mark any standalone type whose state SwiftUI reads or mutates
  (view models, `@Observable` stores) `@MainActor` explicitly, at the type level
  (`@MainActor final class ...`), not per method, unless only some methods need it.
- Cross an actor boundary with `await`; no `@unchecked Sendable` or
  `nonisolated(unsafe)` to make a boundary error disappear. Either the state needs
  no isolation (say so in a comment) or it needs a real actor/lock.

## Sendable conformance

- A value crossing a concurrency boundary (into a `Task`, an actor method, a
  `TaskGroup` child) must be `Sendable`; a `final class` with only immutable
  (`let`) stored properties declares conformance explicitly.
- No `@unchecked Sendable` on a class to silence a diagnostic without auditing
  and documenting why its mutable state cannot race.
- Under Swift 6 language mode, fix a "Sendable" or "actor isolation" error at
  the root (add an actor, make the type immutable, copy before crossing).

## SwiftUI state ownership

- `@State` owns a value the view itself creates: a value type, or (iOS 17+) an
  `@Observable` reference type (`@State private var model = Model()`, not `@StateObject`).
- `@Binding` for a child that reads and mutates a value it does not own.
- `@Environment` for values ambient to a subtree (theme, shared service, feature
  flag); not a shortcut around passing a dependency only one or two views need.
- A view that receives an `@Observable` model and passes a binding to one of its
  properties uses `@Bindable`, not a hand-built `Binding(get:set:)`.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A closure that outlives its creating call (completion handler, Combine `sink`,
  stored callback) capturing `self` strongly: use `[weak self]`, or
  `[unowned self]` only when `self`'s lifetime provably exceeds the closure's.
- Lifting a value into `@State`/`@StateObject` at a view that only displays it
  when a parent owns and mutates it; use `@Binding`/`@Observable` references.
- A view or view-model type that owns navigation, networking and persistence together.
